          "text": "We need answer JSON only, no tools. Need review requirement and graders. Need identify concrete implementation mistakes satisfying graders but violating stated requirement, or grader expectation contradiction. Need at most 4 findings. Need reason deeply.\n\nWe have task: add ${NAME:+word} to python-dotenv. Substitute word only when NAME resolves to nonempty string; otherwise empty. Apply through dotenv_values and load_dotenv preserving override precedence between earlier file values and process env. Keep flat expression grammar: word ends at first closing brace and is not recursively interpolated; no nested expansion or shell eval. Preserve existing ${NAME}, ${NAME:-default}, bare variables, adjacent substitutions, interpolate=False, unassigned keys and malformed-expression behavior. Do not change unrelated parsing or env behavior.\n\nNeed review graders for gaps. We need infer python-dotenv implementation typical. Need know current python-dotenv code? We cannot access omitted source, but can reason. python-dotenv has parser with regex? In recent versions, interpolation uses _posix_regex / _variable? Let's recall: dotenv parser parse_stream yields Binding(key, value, original, error). Variables resolved in resolve_variables using values dict. In main.resolve_variables, for each binding with interpolate True and value not None, atomically uses dotenv.main.resolve_variables? Let's recall exact grammar.\n\npython-dotenv historically supports POSIX variable expansion:\n- _posix_regex = r\"\\${(?P<name>[^\\}:]*)(?P<sep>:?-)?(?P<default>[^}]*)}\" maybe.\nActually src/dotenv/main.py:\n_posix_variable = re.compile(r\"\\$\\{[^\\}]*\\}\")\ndef resolve_variables(values, override=True): new_values = {}; for name, value in values: if value is None: new_values[name] = value; continue; atoms = parse_variables(value); if override: ... Let's recall.\n\ndotenv parser has Original with string, newline. Interpolation likely in main.py:\n_posix_variable: Pattern[str] = re.compile(r\"\\$\\{(?P<name>[^\\}:]*)(?P<sep>:?-)?(?P<default>[^\\}]*)\\}\")\n\ndef resolve_variables(values, override=True):\n    new_values = {}\n    for name, value in values:\n        if value is None:\n            new_values[name] = value\n            continue\n        atoms = parse_variables(value)\n        if override:\n            env = dict(os.environ)\n            env.update(new_values)\n            lookup_order = new_values, env\n        else:\n            lookup_order = env, new_values\n        for atom in atoms:\n            if atom in env? etc\n...\n\nNeed not exact. Grader expectations: public simple BASE=present, OUT chosen. Private alternate checks value present -> chosen, '' -> '', None/missing -> ''. Good.\n\nprecedence: cases with load_dotenv override False/True. env BASE external, file BASE local. OUT=${BASE:+chosen}. Expected table:\n(False, '', 'file', '') : override False, env BASE '' present nonempty? empty, file local 'file' should not override env, so BASE resolves to env '' => empty. Good.\n(True, '', 'file', 'chosen') override True file overrides env empty => BASE file 'file' => chosen.\n(False, 'env', '', 'chosen') env nonempty no override file empty => env => chosen.\n(True, 'env', '', '') override True file empty overrides env nonempty => empty. This tests override precedence including file empty can override env nonempty. Good.\n\nlegacy: os.environ BASE ''. dotenv_values stream A=${BASE:-fallback} -> '' because existing ${NAME:-default}: default only if unset? In shell :- uses default if unset or null. But expected A='' with BASE='' implies python-dotenv treats :- default only when missing, not empty? Need requirement says preserve existing ${NAME:-default}; grader expects A=''. So existing behavior in this task is default when name not defined, not empty. Fine.\n\nliteral: BASE yes OTHER expanded. A=${BASE:+$OTHER} expected '$OTHER' not 'expanded' (word literal not recursively interpolated). B=${BASE:+} word empty? Name nonempty, word empty -> empty. C adjacent substitutions. D=${BASE:+unfinished malformed no closing brace -> literal unchanged. Good.\n\ndisabled-and-unassigned: BASE bare -> None, A=${BASE:+chosen} with BASE=None -> '', B absent -> ''. interpolate False leaves literal.\n\nNeed find concrete implementation mistakes satisfying graders but violating requirement. We need propose wrong impls and distinguishing input required result. Need prioritize realistic errors. Need be specific.\n\nPotential gaps:\n1. Word regex too permissive allowing } inside word via greedy [^}]*? Actually flat grammar word ends at first closing brace. A mistaken implementation could parse ${NAME:+word} using a regex that greedily captures to last closing brace on line, e.g. r\"\\$\\{([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*):\\+(.*)\\}\" greedy would consume first }? Greedy .* before \\} takes last }, so C=x${BASE:+α}${BASE:+β}y would become one expression from first ${ to last } maybe word 'α}${BASE:+β' causing wrong; graders C adjacent would catch if applied. Need satisfy graders but violate. Need hidden cases. A mistake: support + but treat `:-` default as empty-trigger like shell (using `or` truthiness), while grader legacy expects ${BASE:-fallback} -> '' for BASE='' (not shell). Requirement says preserve existing ${NAME:-default}; if existing grader says empty stays empty, wrong implementation changing :- to shell-like would fail grader legacy? It would give A='fallback', fail. Need satisfy graders, so can't.\n\n2. Empty word handling. Requirement: ${BASE:+} with BASE nonempty should substitute empty (word empty). Grader literal B checks BASE=yes => ''. But for BASE empty/missing also empty indistinguishable. A wrong impl might parse `${BASE:+}` as malformed because word empty not allowed and leave literal; grader B catches for nonempty yes. So no.\n\n3. Empty name or invalid names? Requirement mentions NAME resolves to string. Existing grammar? Grader may not test `${:+x}` or `${1:+x}`. A wrong impl could treat empty name as os.environ[''] etc. Need concrete? Maybe less priority.\n\n4. Distinguishing between `+` and `:+`? Task only `${NAME:+word}`; does not require `${NAME+word}` (without colon). A wrong implementation may also support `${NAME+word}` as alternate for defined (even empty) based on regex `:?+`. That would satisfy graders (only :+) but violates \"Do not change unrelated parsing or environment behavior\"/keep flat grammar by adding unrequested operator `${NAME+word}` changing behavior: `${BASE+chosen}` with BASE='' previously maybe literal/malformed, would become chosen/''. Distinguishing input BASE='' A=${BASE+chosen}; required preserve existing behavior (likely unchanged literal) not expand. But is this realistic? Implementer might reuse shell operator table and accidentally include `+`. Confidence medium.\n\n5. Overriding resolution order bug: For dotenv_values, process env should have precedence over earlier file values? Need know python-dotenv existing semantics. Requirement: preserving override precedence between earlier file values and process environment. Private precedence only load_dotenv. dotenv_values has no override parameter? In dotenv_values, interpolate uses existing os.environ and values as parsed. Typical python-dotenv dotenv_values: parse stream then resolve_variables(values, override=True) maybe override True means local values override env? Let's recall dotenv_values(..., interpolate=True) calls resolve_variables(dict, override=True)? In python-dotenv main.dotenv_values: values = parse_stream(stream, interpolate=interpolate); return resolve_variables(values, override=True)? Actually dotenv_values should not change os.environ and interpolation can refer to environment and previous keys. Precedence? In dotenv files, if same key in env and file, load_dotenv override decides. For dotenv_values only returns dict, no override arg, likely file values included regardless; interpolation maybe values in file override env? Public: os.environ.clear, no issue.\n\nPrivate precedence expected for load_dotenv reveals override semantics. A wrong implementation could resolve alternate using final os.environ after assignment rather than sequential earlier file values + env per override, maybe pass graders? Need find violation.\n\n6. Bug: using truthiness of resolved value for `:+`? Should be nonempty string. If value None unassigned -> empty; if value '' -> empty; if value '0' nonempty should substitute. A wrong impl using `if values.get(name):` would treat '0' as nonempty (truthy string) so okay; but if resolved value is not string? Values are str/None only. `bool('0')` true. Fine. If value whitespace ' ' true (nonempty) correct. If implementation uses `.strip()` nonempty, then BASE=' ' -> empty wrong. Graders don't test whitespace. Requirement nonempty string includes whitespace-only as nonempty. Distinguishing input BASE=' ' OUT=${BASE:+chosen}; expected chosen, wrong strip gives ''. This is concrete and realistic (dev checks `if value and value.strip()`), satisfies all graders (no whitespace). High? Good finding.\n\n7. Bug: treating word as recursively interpolated. Requirement says not recursively interpolated; grader literal A expects '$OTHER'. So recursive would fail grader. Not a gap.\n\n8. Bug: parse expression by `value.replace('${' + name + ':+'...)`? Need specific.\n\n9. Malformed expression behavior: D=${BASE:+unfinished no close remains literal. Grader covers. Missing closing with later closing? e.g. A=${BASE:+x} and B=... no.\n\n10. Flat grammar: word ends at first closing brace. A wrong implementation could allow escaped braces or nested brace matching (match last brace) to support words containing }. This would satisfy graders (words no braces except D malformed no close, C adjacent maybe if match last brace fails? C has adjacent with no nested braces; greedy to last would fail C as above; non-greedy first ok). Hidden input: BASE=yes; A=${BASE:+a}b}c. Required word ends at first } => 'ab' then 'c' => 'abc